Q: Vite + React + TypeScript failing to resolve @/components import path A: <p>Can you post your vite.config.ts?</p>
<p>I guess you need resolve alias, something like this:</p>
<pre><code>import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue';
import { resolve } from 'path';
// https://vitejs.dev/config/
export default defineConfig({
  plugins: [vue()],
  resolve: {
    alias: {
      '@': resolve(__dirname, './src'),
      '@components': resolve(__dirname, './src/components'),
      '@pages': resolve(__dirname, './src/pages'),
    },
  },
});
</code></pre>
<p>This way you can import your AppLayout like</p>
<pre><code>import AppLayout from '@/components/layout/AppLayout'
</code></pre>
<p>or</p>
<pre><code>import AppLayout from '@components/layout/AppLayout'
</code></pre>

Q: Terraform ELB access_log S3 access Permissions Issue A: <p>Older AWS regions before August 2022 required granting permission to a <strong>region-specific AWS account root</strong> because Elastic Load Balancing delivered access logs using region-owned AWS accounts.</p>
<pre><code>{
            &quot;Sid&quot;: &quot;AWSConsoleStmt-887673773838&quot;,
            &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,
            &quot;Principal&quot;: {
                &quot;AWS&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:iam::{aws_region-id}:root&quot;
            },
            &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;,
            &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>AWS has since moved to a <strong>service principal–based model</strong> (logdelivery.elasticloadbalancing.amazonaws.com) to improve security and consistency.</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;Version&quot;:&quot;2012-10-17&quot;,                
  &quot;Statement&quot;: [
    {
      &quot;Effect&quot;: &quot;Allow&quot;,
      &quot;Principal&quot;: {
        &quot;Service&quot;: &quot;logdelivery.elasticloadbalancing.amazonaws.com&quot;
      },
      &quot;Action&quot;: &quot;s3:PutObject&quot;,
      &quot;Resource&quot;: &quot;arn:aws:s3:::bucket-name/*&quot;
    }
  ]
}
</code></pre>
<p>Reference : <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/enable-access-logs.html#attach-bucket-policy" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://docs.aws.amazon.com/elasticloadbalancing/latest/classic/enable-access-logs.html#attach-bucket-policy</a></p>

Q: Why int[] Array is not reliable as Java HashMap Key A: <p>Let's take it step by step. Yes, you can use an array as a key... <em>even if it doesn't make any sense</em>.<br />
Let's look at the “why?” with an example:</p>
<pre><code>Integer a[] = { 1, 2 };
Integer b[] = { 1, 2 };
System.out.println( a.equals( b ) );
</code></pre>
<p>The previous statement prints <em>&quot;false&quot;</em>, as you have been told in the comments (even the false “AI”, which I would not ask anything), the class <strong>“[]”</strong> does not override the <strong>equals()</strong> method, so it only verifies that it is the same object (that the pointer points to the same place in memory).<br />
This may not seem like a big problem, since if I use the same array to create the entry and get the value, it should work, and in fact it does:</p>
<pre><code>Map &lt;int[], String&gt; mp = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
int keyA[] = new int[] { 1, 2 };
mp.put( keyA, &quot;Work&quot; );
System.out.println( mp.get( keyA ));
</code></pre>
<p>Very nice, he even printed <em>“Work”</em>, everything seems fine, but...</p>
<pre><code>Map &lt;int[], String&gt; mp = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
int keyA[] = new int[] { 1, 2 };
int keyB[] = new int[] { 1, 2 };
mp.put( keyA, &quot;Work&quot; );

if( mp.get( keyB ) == null ) 
   System.out.println( &quot;Not work&quot; );
</code></pre>
<p>And it does indeed print <em>“Not work”</em>, here we confirm that two arrays with the same content are not interchangeable as <em>&quot;key&quot;</em>, but we haven't seen the worst yet...</p>
<pre><code>Map &lt;int[], String&gt; mp = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
int keyA[] = new int[] { 1,2 };
int keyB[] = new int[] { 1,2 };
mp.put( keyA, &quot;Work&quot; );

keyA[ 0 ] = 14;
System.out.println( mp.get( keyA ));
</code></pre>
<p>How awful! We have modified the contents of the array, and it keeps printing <em>“Work”</em>...</p>
<p>Conclusion:
Although you can use them... don't, and if you decide to use them anyway, don't waste resources loading values into them; an empty array works exactly the same.</p>
<pre><code>Map &lt;int[], String&gt; mp = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
int keyA[] = new int[2];
int keyB[] = new int[2];
mp.put( keyA, &quot;Work&quot; );

System.out.println( mp.get( keyA ));

if( mp.get( keyB ) == null ) 
   System.out.println( &quot;Not work&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
